  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front will approach the region on Tuesday and generally\nremain mostly to our south through Tuesday evening before\npossible lifting northward into southern NH and perhaps coastal\nME. Rapid moisture return is expected by Tuesday evening with\nforecast soundings showing PWATS rising to around 2 inches...and\neven higher in some spots. The main risk will be convection\narriving late Tuesday afternoon or early evening in the western\nzones, then pushing eastward to the rest of the forecast area\nduring the evening hours. There is a low risk for a few wind\nproducing storms given the strong low level jet. However, the\nmain threat will be torrential downpours with an attendant risk\nfor isolated instances of flash flooding Tuesday evening into\nthe overnight hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWednesday, broad low pressure remains to the northwest of the \nforecast area, attempting to vertically stack across portions of\nwestern Quebec. Southern Maine and all of New Hampshire will \nreside in a warm sector, but primary moisture transport will \nhave moved north or off into the Gulf of Maine. The trough axis \nnears later in the day, with a cold front forecast to move \nacross much of the forecast area by mid evening. \n\nThis front will be the primary forcing agent for possible \nthunderstorm development. Amid a well sheared environment and \nensembles supportive of up to 1000 j/kg of instability, some of \nthese storms could be strong to severe. REFS hodographs are \nstraight, with 35-40 kts of 0-6km shear and a IQR of 0-1km shear\naround 15 to 18kts. This is towards the end of the 12z REFS \ntime range, so expect some changes in magnitude, but shear is \nthe higher confidence variable at this range when comparing to \ninstability. Given this setup, storms may congregate along the \nfrontal forcing, but also can't rule out some isolated \nconvection ahead of it. Shear would keep this organization \npossible as long as there is instability to muster. Any deeper \nstorms would have the capability to produce gusty winds, with \nhail more likely in any discrete cells.\n\nThere are a few caveats to this setup:\n\nDry air will be more prevalent after the initial warm front \nsurge on Tuesday. PWATs fall to a range of 0.75 inch to 1.5 inch\nacross the forecast area Wednesday(the greater amounts reside \ntowards the coast). This could keep coverage low, and perhaps \nonly along the front.\n\nBecause air source region may be from the Upper Midwest, will \nneed to factor in the potential for smoke to move in where the \nexpected instability is to develop. HRRR Smoke model shows this \npossibility, although the concentration weakens towards the end \nof the range. This could be correct, or it could be a limitation\nof adding smoke to the model. Tend to trust the overall \ntrajectory more at this time, so felt this possibility was worth\nmentioning as a caveat. \n\nInto the evening, loss of instability is expected and will lead\nto a lower storm/precip threat. Winds gradually shift WNW \novernight. A period of quieter weather is then expected Thursday\nand Friday as strong high pressure arrives from the Great \nLakes.",
